::STAT_U06_L04_GP_Q01::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L04_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.PSU.14
Question:
A student answers 10 questions. Success means correct, and the probability of success is 0.70. What is n?

{
=A. 10#Correct. n is the number of trials, which is 10 questions.
~B. 0.70#This is p, the probability of success.
~C. Correct#This names the success outcome, not n.
~D. 7#This is the expected number of successes, not the number of trials.
}

::STAT_U06_L04_GP_Q02::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L04_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.PSU.14
Question:
In a binomial setting, what does p represent?

{
~A. The number of trials#The number of trials is n.
=B. The probability of success on one trial#Correct. p is the chance of success for each trial.
~C. The number of failures#Failure count is not represented by p.
~D. The total expected value formula#p is part of the formula, not the entire formula.
}

::STAT_U06_L04_GP_Q03::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L04_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.PSU.14
Question:
A basketball player takes 20 shots and makes each shot with probability 0.40. What is the expected number of made shots?

{
~A. 0.40#This is the probability of one success, not expected successes.
~B. 4#This uses 0.20 instead of 0.40.
=C. 8#Correct. n × p = 20 × 0.40 = 8.
~D. 20#This assumes every shot is made.
}

::STAT_U06_L04_GP_Q04::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L04_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.PSU.14
Question:
Which situation is most clearly binomial?

{
~A. Recording the exact temperature every hour#Temperature has many possible values, not just success/failure.
~B. Measuring the height of students#Height is quantitative, not a success/failure trial.
~C. Asking students to name their favorite class#There are more than two possible categories.
=D. Counting how many of 15 emails are opened#Correct. Each email is opened or not opened, and successes are counted.
}

::STAT_U06_L04_GP_Q05::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L04_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.PSU.14
Question:
What does an expected value of 6 successes mean in a binomial context?

{
=A. Over many repeated sets of trials, the average number of successes is about 6#Correct. Expected value is a long-run average.
~B. Exactly 6 successes must happen every time#Expected value does not guarantee one trial set.
~C. The probability of success is 6#A probability cannot be 6.
~D. There are only 6 trials#Expected successes and number of trials are not always the same.
}
